            {
              "id": "5677da5e-1211-4558-8306-693443615cc9",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "html": "<strong>The Life of a Sportsman</strong> was written by Charles James “Nimrod” Apperley, published by Eclipse Sporting Gallery in 1842."
            },
            {
              "id": "03d0bde4-a03c-4549-b2ff-f9f93e456002",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "html": ""
            },
            {
              "id": "7a2c11b8-957c-4870-a1bc-e7b391f8a7e5",
              "type": "quote",
              "html": "<em>&quot;When Lockhart said of 'Nimrod' that he could 'hunt like Hugo Meynell and write like Walter Scott,' he was doubtless excited into exaggeration by the pleasure of having hit upon a man who could write of sport without the vulgarity of Egan...The Life of a Sportsman, published in 1842, contains a very pleasant account of country life in days when sport was no longer confused with debauchery; while its descriptions of runs to hounds, its lore of hunting and of four-in-hand driving, and its variety of incident and anecdote make it still both valuable and agreeable. Apperley, though not a Walter Scott, was a good writer; he knew his subject thoroughly, on both the scientific and the personal sides, and this work of fiction, though poor in plot, is rich in interest&quot;</em> (Cambridge History of English and American Literature, Volume XIV. The Victorian Age, Part Two. Ch. 15, Nimrod)"
            },
            {
              "id": "e53af843-f597-43c7-ad77-8b978b361bb9",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "html": ""
            }
